01:39:18.610 --> 01:39:24.010
yes sir the actors that were with us in the fda show peter boyle howard hessman

01:39:24.010 --> 01:39:29.870
gary goodrow they all agreed to be in it too jules feiffer did some of the wrote some of the

01:39:30.000 --> 01:39:37.710
skits. Herb Gardner, Freddie Gardner, Barbara Garson, I mean, you know, really good writers

01:39:37.710 --> 01:39:50.630
put together a bunch of skits, and we rehearsed. And in March 1971, we did our first show at

01:39:50.630 --> 01:39:58.520
the Haymarket Square Coffee House in Fort Bragg, North Carolina. I mean, it was just awesome.

01:39:58.520 --> 01:39:59.920
There were reporters there from

01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:04.950
all the major newspapers and the headlines were wild you know when when

01:40:05.000 --> 01:40:12.260
Barbara Dane sang join the GI movement boys join the GI movement I mean like

01:40:12.260 --> 01:40:18.460
five hundred guys rose to their feet fists in the air oh I know who else was

01:40:18.460 --> 01:40:27.660
there Dick Gregory was there and it was a huge success FTA is an acronym that

01:40:27.660 --> 01:40:28.000
means

01:40:30.570 --> 01:40:36.520
F the army, we always said free the army, or fun travel and adventure, but it really

01:40:36.520 --> 01:40:42.010
meant the army, and it was a GI favorite saying.

01:40:42.430 --> 01:40:50.120
So it was called the FTA show, and the military officers always tried to keep the guys away.

01:40:50.340 --> 01:40:56.270
They would, you know, infrared cameras, clear obvious plain clothes, everything to harass

01:40:56.270 --> 01:40:59.550
them and make them feel, man, if we go in here, we're going to get in trouble, and still, we

01:40:59.550 --> 01:40:59.970
We did three.

01:41:00.000 --> 01:41:06.580
standing room only performances it was it was great we did it in the coffee

01:41:06.580 --> 01:41:11.940
house yes we we applied we requested to come on base but we weren't allowed to

01:41:13.200 --> 01:41:19.940
and we finally did a performance at to raise money for to fund the show we did

01:41:19.940 --> 01:41:25.320
it at Philharmonic Hall and Nina Simone was was there and then she came with us

01:41:25.320 --> 01:41:26.600
when we went to Fort Dix

01:41:37.130 --> 01:41:39.310
It won an Obie Award, Off-Broadway Award,

01:41:39.530 --> 01:41:42.540
for our performance at Philharmonic Hall, yes.

01:41:43.250 --> 01:41:46.960
And we performed, you know, when we started out,

01:41:48.840 --> 01:41:53.260
we were focusing on Army, Navy, and Marines.

01:41:53.400 --> 01:41:55.320
There was a huge movement in the Navy.

01:41:55.520 --> 01:41:57.900
It was called, well, the Coral Sea,

01:41:58.000 --> 01:41:59.980
which was a big aircraft carrier, had a...

01:42:00.000 --> 01:42:04.780
movement called SOS stop our ship and there were thousands of guys sailors on

01:42:04.780 --> 01:42:09.260
the on the on the Coral Sea that signed a petition against the war. What is it

01:42:09.260 --> 01:42:15.040
that that started you all thinking and how did you come to understand the role

01:42:15.040 --> 01:42:21.100
that an attack carrier plays? I suppose everybody always knows but they they can

01:42:21.100 --> 01:42:25.550
rationalize anything away that's man's you know the way his mind works. Like you

01:42:25.550 --> 01:42:29.410
read the polls and people want us to get out of Vietnam and you see the polls

01:42:29.410 --> 01:42:29.930
getting higher.

01:42:30.000 --> 01:42:35.560
and higher every week, but yet we aren't out, you know, and how can you rationalize this?

01:42:35.660 --> 01:42:59.760
There was quite a lot of activity in the, among, you know, in the Navy, but we thought, well, the Air Force, I mean, there's just, there's no way, you know, the Air Force is the elite, you know, the officers, well, one of the unanticipated results of Nixon's Vietnamization program was the war moved from being a ground war because the troops were coming home in order to quell the anti-war movement.

01:43:00.670 --> 01:43:07.010
was more bombs dropped, more heinous kinds of bombs

01:43:07.010 --> 01:43:08.890
were used than had ever been used before

01:43:08.890 --> 01:43:13.450
that are outlawed according to the Geneva laws of war,

01:43:13.570 --> 01:43:14.230
rules of war.

01:43:15.490 --> 01:43:17.730
There were more planes being shot down,

01:43:17.830 --> 01:43:20.070
more pilots being taken prisoner.

01:43:20.940 --> 01:43:23.320
And so suddenly, something that we hadn't anticipated

01:43:23.320 --> 01:43:26.360
was that the GI movement began to enter the ranks

01:43:26.360 --> 01:43:29.940
of the Air Force, massively in the spring of 19-

01:43:31.800 --> 01:43:37.030
There were riots, Travis Air Force Base, all over the country at Air Forces Base.

01:43:37.170 --> 01:43:39.440
I mean, it was just, it was amazing.

01:43:39.660 --> 01:43:49.040
For the first time, a GI coffee house opened up in Mountain Home, Idaho, by the Mountain Home Air Force Base.

01:43:49.320 --> 01:43:55.450
That was the only Air Force Base that the FTA show played at stateside, domestically.

01:43:57.860 --> 01:43:59.840
Country Joe McDonald was there for that.

01:44:00.510 --> 01:44:04.310
We had a lot of wonderful celebrities that would join us, you know, along the way.

01:44:04.550 --> 01:44:17.640
And then what happened in the spring of 71, I was starting to get, I think it's a phase that you go through when you're new to the movement.

01:44:17.640 --> 01:44:19.900
I was starting to get very politically correct.

01:44:20.600 --> 01:44:29.640
I started to feel this is too white because I was realizing how many people of color there were in the military.

01:44:30.670 --> 01:44:36.580
And except for the times that Dick Gregory would come and join us, we were all white.

01:44:37.420 --> 01:44:39.400
And except for me, we were all male.

01:44:43.470 --> 01:44:49.920
Well, and I was going through my humorless, pedantic, politically correct phase.

01:44:50.660 --> 01:44:58.680
And of course, that was a big turnoff to Alan Meyerson and Peter Boyle and Gary Goodrow and Howard Hessman.

01:45:00.000 --> 01:45:02.960
So it was decided to amicably part ways,

01:45:02.960 --> 01:45:05.840
and we began to restructure and rewrite the show.

01:45:07.860 --> 01:45:12.020
And we ended up with four men, four women,

01:45:12.740 --> 01:45:14.180
two black women, two white women,

01:45:14.340 --> 01:45:15.540
two black men, two white men.

01:45:15.780 --> 01:45:17.360
We said,

01:45:17.560 --> 01:45:19.440
Foxtrot, Tango, Alpha.

01:45:20.040 --> 01:45:29.370
It was really good that we got.

01:45:30.510 --> 01:45:34.880
black men and black women because when we finally went overseas

01:45:35.480 --> 01:45:41.550
the black gis wanted to meet with blacks i feel all black men should be exempt from

01:45:41.550 --> 01:45:44.400
our military duty anyway because uh

01:45:45.000 --> 01:45:48.000
right off the only place a black man should fight

01:45:48.580 --> 01:45:52.320
where he's being oppressed i'm not being oppressed in japan i'm not being

01:45:52.320 --> 01:45:55.280
oppressed in vietnam and i'm not being oppressed in pakistan

01:45:55.280 --> 01:45:59.000
during the summer of 72 we did a lot of fundraising

01:46:00.220 --> 01:46:04.500
We performed the FTA show, I remember, in the backyard of Quincy Jones' house.

01:46:04.960 --> 01:46:07.060
And Ben Vereen joined us on that one.

01:46:08.970 --> 01:46:12.590
And we raised enough money, and I wrote a letter, Dear President Nixon,

01:46:13.920 --> 01:46:17.440
asking permission to take our show, you know, to South Vietnam,

01:46:17.700 --> 01:46:20.400
to perform for the soldiers like Bob Hope does.

01:46:20.500 --> 01:46:22.840
And, you know, I wasn't holding my breath for a Dear Jane,

01:46:22.960 --> 01:46:24.720
we'd love to have you please come over.

01:46:24.820 --> 01:46:26.900
But I wanted to say that I did it.

01:46:27.200 --> 01:46:28.360
Of course, we were denied.

01:46:30.000 --> 01:46:31.200
In fact, I don't even think I heard back.

01:46:32.230 --> 01:46:35.770
But we raised the money to do this overseas tour.

01:46:36.700 --> 01:46:45.000
And we started in Hawaii, and then the Philippines, and then we were going to go to Japan.

01:46:45.800 --> 01:46:47.580
But we were not allowed in.

01:46:48.490 --> 01:46:51.650
They stopped us at customs, because they said we didn't have the right visas.

01:46:51.790 --> 01:46:53.630
Well, it was the Pentagon was putting pressure on us.

01:47:00.000 --> 01:47:05.000
for a special inquiry and nobody else is obviously going to surrender a passport so I'm just I'm very

01:47:05.000 --> 01:47:12.190
curious are you getting through we're not getting through and so we changed our itinerary and we

01:47:12.190 --> 01:47:19.110
went to Okinawa and while we were in Okinawa the government because it made headlines all over the

01:47:19.110 --> 01:47:24.230
place the fact that we'd been turned away in Japan and the government was so I think concerned the

01:47:24.230 --> 01:47:29.410
Pentagon was so concerned that they we'd gotten all this publicity that they let us in so when

01:47:29.410 --> 01:47:29.890
when we finish.

01:47:30.000 --> 01:47:36.730
in okinawa we we then went on to japan and we ended the tour in japan we did 21 performances

01:47:37.530 --> 01:47:46.570
for 64 000 servicemen and women in two weeks finishing on christmas day and it was it was

01:47:46.620 --> 01:47:56.720
wild it was wild the brass the officers the you know the the military powers that be they

01:47:56.720 --> 01:47:59.960
they would issue news releases giving the wrong time and the

01:48:00.000 --> 01:48:06.470
wrong place, where we were going to be performing, you know, threats, all kinds of things. And we

01:48:06.470 --> 01:48:11.800
would simply wait. We'd wait until they showed up. And sometimes they showed up 5,000, 8,000. I mean,

01:48:12.560 --> 01:48:20.180
when we went to Okinawa, they put a curfew. And so we just stayed and played for a thousand guys

01:48:20.180 --> 01:48:24.840
each time. We just played several performances so they could all end up coming. We've just been

01:48:24.840 --> 01:48:29.060
figuring out what to do about the fact that there are apparently several thousand more GIs outside

01:48:29.060 --> 01:48:29.920
that can't come in.

01:48:30.000 --> 01:48:37.840
and so we're going to do a second show and one of the ways that the show was

01:48:37.840 --> 01:48:44.260
different than it was in the beginning was we dealt a lot more with the

01:48:44.830 --> 01:48:49.290
hierarchy within the military because that was a big issue for soldiers you

01:48:49.290 --> 01:48:53.650
know if you were an enlisted man's wife and you were having a miscarriage you

01:48:53.650 --> 01:48:58.760
might not get medical care whereas if you were an officer's wife well do you

01:48:58.760 --> 01:48:59.680
You know, that, that.

01:49:00.090 --> 01:49:00.790
Sort of thing.

01:49:01.430 --> 01:49:04.210
What's your husband's rank?

01:49:05.830 --> 01:49:06.790
He was.

01:49:07.580 --> 01:49:09.760
Come back and see us when he makes sergeant.

01:49:10.300 --> 01:49:13.740
They loved the fact that we had done the research,

01:49:13.740 --> 01:49:16.820
that we knew what their issues were,

01:49:17.910 --> 01:49:19.350
you know, what really got to them,

01:49:19.350 --> 01:49:21.630
and we did skits about it,

01:49:21.630 --> 01:49:24.510
and they just, oh, God, they just loved it.

01:49:24.570 --> 01:49:26.710
And Rita Martinson wrote a song called

01:49:26.710 --> 01:49:28.980
Dear Soldier, We Love You,

01:49:29.000 --> 01:49:29.980
and she has such a...

01:49:30.000 --> 01:49:59.020
soulful voice. I read that you took a stand and refused to kill in Vietnam. I used to love to watch the faces of the G.I.s when she sang that. It was like this shell of tension would drop away and you would see the youth and the innocence and the vulnerability underneath. There was a guy I remember one night, it was in, I think it was in.

01:50:02.490 --> 01:50:04.270
I don't remember if it was the Philippines or Japan,

01:50:04.630 --> 01:50:07.570
he had a body cast on that covered everything

01:50:07.570 --> 01:50:11.460
except his face and tears were running down his cheeks.

01:50:14.610 --> 01:50:17.550
You know, it was a spit in a prayer kind of production.

01:50:18.670 --> 01:50:23.140
What was hard for me was,

01:50:24.280 --> 01:50:26.560
because Barbarella had just been playing, you know,

01:50:27.760 --> 01:50:29.980
I was a pinup.

01:50:30.540 --> 01:50:33.560
You know, and it's possible that a lot of them came to see Barbarella,

01:50:34.270 --> 01:50:39.170
and they had posters of me as Barbarella or, you know, sex kitten.

01:50:39.530 --> 01:50:45.890
And when I came out with no makeup and jeans and everything,

01:50:46.970 --> 01:50:48.770
you could tell they were disappointed.

01:50:48.890 --> 01:50:59.620
And it scared me because I knew that it was important for me to continue.

01:51:00.640 --> 01:51:03.580
to have a film career because that's where my clout came from.

01:51:04.360 --> 01:51:10.940
And that's why I could do what I could do in the movement, but I didn't know if anyone

01:51:10.940 --> 01:51:13.000
would ever want to see me in a movie again.

01:51:13.420 --> 01:51:26.760
And Holly Neer, one of the guys said to her, I got so mad at her that I tore the Barbarella

01:51:26.760 --> 01:51:27.580
poster down.

01:51:30.000 --> 01:51:32.180
But other guys said, we put up FTA posters.

01:51:33.240 --> 01:51:37.020
You know, but I could tell there was that anger, you know.

01:51:37.160 --> 01:51:43.710
And I wasn't mature enough politically to do what I should have done.

01:51:44.480 --> 01:51:49.490
I should have walked out there and I should have said, I know you guys are disappointed.

01:51:49.670 --> 01:51:52.230
But let me tell you something.

01:51:53.560 --> 01:51:55.120
Sexual fantasies are great.

01:51:55.220 --> 01:51:59.970
But when you're the object of the fantasy, it's hard.

01:52:01.140 --> 01:52:03.720
You're objectified, like G.I.s are.

01:52:04.460 --> 01:52:06.960
You've just been made into cogs in a wheel.

01:52:07.220 --> 01:52:08.160
You're cannon fodder.

01:52:08.520 --> 01:52:11.440
It's the same kind of thing.

01:52:11.440 --> 01:52:12.120
Do you understand?

01:52:12.700 --> 01:52:16.980
I need right now to try to get back my humanity,

01:52:16.980 --> 01:52:20.000
which means shedding that Barbarella stuff.

01:52:20.530 --> 01:52:22.230
You know, don't resent me for it.

01:52:22.290 --> 01:52:23.770
Understand where I'm coming from.

01:52:23.910 --> 01:52:27.650
And I know they would have if I'd been able to be honest about it

01:52:27.650 --> 01:52:29.960
and I'd been able to say,

01:52:30.000 --> 01:52:36.280
I know, I know you're mad and I understand. I have my own sexual fantasies, you know, but

01:52:37.940 --> 01:52:42.760
it dehumanizes us. We don't want to be dehumanized. You don't want to be dehumanized by the military

01:52:42.760 --> 01:52:49.010
and I don't want to be dehumanized by sexual fantasies. That's not who I am. And they would

01:52:49.010 --> 01:52:52.230
have gotten it, but I didn't know to do that. My life would have been different if I'd known

01:52:52.230 --> 01:52:59.980
how to do that then. Anyway, it was, it was just, it was the

01:53:00.000 --> 01:53:01.640
The entire thing was an amazing experience.

01:53:01.800 --> 01:53:04.640
Holly Neer, we had to replace an actress singer

01:53:04.640 --> 01:53:07.000
at the last minute and Holly Neer came and auditioned

01:53:07.000 --> 01:53:09.080
and knocked my socks off.

01:53:09.180 --> 01:53:10.200
I mean, does she have a voice?

01:53:10.520 --> 01:53:12.240
She was very, very young.

01:53:12.480 --> 01:53:14.280
She thought military industrial complex

01:53:14.280 --> 01:53:15.360
was a metal disorder.

01:53:15.460 --> 01:53:19.020
We've got a lot of bases and we're fighting for places

01:53:19.020 --> 01:53:21.780
as our interests increase.

01:53:23.710 --> 01:53:26.890
In the Philippines, we had a great interpreter

01:53:27.710 --> 01:53:28.870
because all the local

01:53:30.190 --> 01:53:35.010
you know, stops, there would be local organizers that were supportive of the GI movement that

01:53:35.010 --> 01:53:37.190
would join up with us and act as translators.

01:53:37.650 --> 01:53:39.380
She did a remarkable thing.

01:53:39.560 --> 01:53:50.400
She brought in a group of Philippine prostitutes who talked to us about how they would, the

01:53:50.400 --> 01:53:58.160
boats would come in, and they would, you know, get their johns, and they would sleep with

01:53:58.160 --> 01:53:59.040
the guys for money.

01:54:00.000 --> 01:54:03.740
And they needed money because their families had been put out of work, because their farms

01:54:03.740 --> 01:54:07.080
had been paved over for military bases and so forth.

01:54:07.460 --> 01:54:10.800
And then they would tell the GIs why the war was wrong.

01:54:12.330 --> 01:54:16.330
They would pitch an anti-war message to the soldiers that they just slept with.

01:54:16.450 --> 01:54:19.600
I mean, I did, you know, wild, wild.

01:54:19.780 --> 01:54:26.370
When we pulled into Subic Bay, the Coral Sea had what we knew, the Coral Sea was loaded

01:54:26.370 --> 01:54:29.610
with guys that were part of the Stop Our Ship SOS movement.

01:54:30.000 --> 01:54:31.980
But they were kept out, they weren't allowed to land.

01:54:33.570 --> 01:54:37.530
And as we pulled into, drove into Subic Bay,

01:54:37.670 --> 01:54:42.350
which is the main town of the U.S. Naval base

01:54:42.350 --> 01:54:45.250
in the Philippines, the streets were lined

01:54:45.250 --> 01:54:49.010
with these little girls that would pull up there

01:54:49.580 --> 01:54:50.500
and offer themselves.

01:54:51.290 --> 01:54:52.870
The only hotel we could find,

01:54:53.190 --> 01:54:54.950
rooms could only be booked for an hour.

01:54:55.250 --> 01:54:56.770
Then you could push a button when you were ready

01:54:56.770 --> 01:54:57.810
to have a girl come up.

01:54:57.910 --> 01:54:59.930
I mean, we had to bribe the guy to let us stay overnight.

01:55:00.000 --> 01:55:06.970
it was this is the culture we're exporting I mean this is what this is

01:55:06.970 --> 01:55:13.730
what we're doing Hawaii same thing lands paved over corporate America kind of

01:55:14.780 --> 01:55:18.680
putting local people out of business a tremendous amount of resentment it's so

01:55:18.680 --> 01:55:24.240
painful when you're an American to hear this and we heard it everywhere we

01:55:24.330 --> 01:55:29.760
didn't hear it in Japan we heard it in Okinawa where basically the whole island

01:55:30.000 --> 01:55:34.520
taken over by the U.S. military, and we heard it in Hawaii, and we heard it in the Philippines,

01:55:34.720 --> 01:55:37.040
and it was just, it was really hard to take.

01:55:37.200 --> 01:55:43.000
And for those of us like Holly and me, Michael Alaimo was, you know, he was not new to him,

01:55:43.180 --> 01:55:51.020
but I'd never, I'd never had this concept of U.S. imperialism had flesh and blood put

01:55:51.020 --> 01:55:59.190
on it, faces put on it, my father is starving because his farm was paved over, face put

01:55:59.190 --> 01:55:59.610
on it.

01:56:00.640 --> 01:56:02.600
And it was hard. It was hard to take.

01:56:02.860 --> 01:56:08.280
We demanded an immediate and total withdrawal of all air and ground troops and CIA from Vietnam,

01:56:08.950 --> 01:56:18.370
as well as from Korea, Guam, Okinawa, Japan, the Philippines, Israel, Cambodia, Thailand, Germany, England, Panama, Japan.

01:56:18.750 --> 01:56:28.710
I mean, it seems unthinkable now that we could have done this and that you could have a hall full of guys.

01:56:30.160 --> 01:56:31.620
with their fists in the air.

01:56:32.680 --> 01:56:39.180
So happy that we had come to acknowledge the reality.

01:56:52.420 --> 01:56:53.620
We're not going to Vietnam.

01:56:53.920 --> 01:56:55.200
We're refusing our orders.

01:56:55.320 --> 01:56:57.500
And in fact, we're resigning from the military.

01:56:57.720 --> 01:56:58.480
Come and get it.

01:57:03.740 --> 01:57:06.120
I just understand that I liked being a Green Beret.

01:57:07.240 --> 01:57:08.480
I thought it was good.

01:57:09.520 --> 01:57:16.330
The problem I had was realizing that what I was doing was not doing it right.

01:57:16.550 --> 01:57:17.980
I wasn't doing it right.

01:57:21.780 --> 01:57:26.120
During the Vietnam War, an anti-war movement emerged that altered the course of history.

01:57:26.580 --> 01:57:29.960
This movement didn't take place on college campuses, but in barracks.

01:57:30.030 --> 01:57:35.270
and on ships. It flourished in army stockades, navy brigs, and the dingy towns that surround

01:57:35.270 --> 01:57:41.090
the military bases. Hundreds went to prison and thousands into exile, and by 1971 it had,

01:57:41.130 --> 01:57:44.460
in the words of one colonel, infested the entire armed services.

01:57:45.040 --> 01:57:47.710
Mr. President, there's a terror demonstration going on outside.

01:57:48.190 --> 01:57:50.930
Oh, there's always a demonstration going on outside, Pat.

01:57:51.070 --> 01:57:54.250
But Richard, this one is completely out of control. They're storming the White House.

01:57:54.270 --> 01:57:55.850
Oh, in that case, I better call out the Third Marines.

01:57:55.870 --> 01:57:56.730
You can't, Richard.

01:57:56.890 --> 01:57:57.070
Why not?

01:57:57.090 --> 01:57:58.190
It is the Third Marines.

01:58:00.000 --> 01:58:04.450
nothing to lose it's a really free place you don't know what's going to happen you know where

01:58:04.450 --> 01:58:08.390
you're going but you know what you're doing within two days of hitting the stockade i was

01:58:08.390 --> 01:58:14.470
facing the death sentence forsaking we shall overcome this man didn't do me nothing he heard

01:58:14.470 --> 01:58:19.160
none of my black people know my family so why should i show you a new phenomenon is cropped

01:58:19.160 --> 01:58:24.760
up at several army bases these days a so-called underground gi press there must have been close

01:58:24.760 --> 01:58:29.940
